.TH IPTS 4
.SH NAME
ipts \- a Surface's touch screen (Intel Precise Touch & Stylus)
.SH SYNOPSIS
No port: it sends touches to the console.
x86-64 only.
.B /bin/ipts
is started at boot by
.BR init (8),
with device rights; with no iTouch MEI, or one that will not start (3
tries), it ends with status 0 after one line saying why, and is not started
again.
.SH DESCRIPTION
A Surface's touch controller is not a HID device the host sees: Intel's
Management Engine runs it, and talks to SIEOS through its own MEI function,
the "iTouch" one (Ice Lake: PCI 00:16.4, 8086:34e4; Kaby Lake: 8086:9d3e).
.PP
.B ipts
resets the MEI (two rings of 32-bit slots, polled), asks the bus for its
clients and connects to IPTS's (a fixed-address client is used without
connecting), gets the sensor's information, gives it 16 data buffers and 16
feedback buffers in DMA memory, then reads each buffer the sensor fills and
gives it back.
On the Surface Pro 4 to 7 the Management Engine turns the sensor's frames
into a single-touch HID report, which user/lib/hidrep.c reads as a touch
screen: the console gets an absolute position
.RB ( CON_INJECT ),
the left button while touching.
.PP
Every step is logged (the serial console,
.BR dmesg (1)).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR con (4),
.BR i2chid (4)
.SH BUGS
One finger only: several fingers and the pen need the raw mode (heat maps,
the pen's own reports), not done.
